#import "/shared/book.typ": *
#let p = problem.with(code: "MM7033", unit: "U6")
#let items = (
 p(1,"E","U6.1 Localized integers",[Describe $ZZ[1/3]$ as a subring of $QQ$.],[The allowed denominators are powers of three.],[Fractions may simplify but need not have a unique presentation.],[It is $\{a/3^n:a in ZZ,n>=0\}$. Addition and multiplication keep this form. It is the smallest subring of $QQ$ containing $ZZ$ and $1/3$.],[Specify allowed denominators.]),
 p(2,"E","U6.1 Units at a prime",[Which of $2,5,10$ are units in $ZZ_((5))$?],[Denominators not divisible by five are inverted.],[An integer is a unit exactly when five does not divide it.],[Two is a unit, with inverse $1/2$. Five and ten lie in the unique maximal ideal and are not units. If either had an inverse with denominator not divisible by five, cross multiplication would force that denominator divisible by five.],[Justify the nonunit claims.]),
 p(3,"E","U6.1 Vanishing",[In $ZZ slash 10ZZ$, localize at powers of two. Show the class of five becomes zero.],[Use the localization vanishing criterion.],[Two times five is zero in the original ring.],[The element two belongs to the denominator set and annihilates five. Hence $[5]/1=0$ in the localization by the defining fraction relation.],[Do not cancel two inside the original ring.]),
 p(4,"E","U6.2 Localizing a cyclic module",[Compute $ZZ[1/2]otimes_ZZ ZZ slash 8ZZ$.],[Use quotient by eight times the localized ring.],[Eight is now a unit.],[The tensor is $ZZ[1/2] slash 8ZZ[1/2]=0$, since multiplication by eight is invertible in the localized ring.],[Explain why the quotient is zero.]),
 p(5,"C","U6.1 Injectivity criterion",[Prove the map $R -> S^(-1)R$ is injective exactly when no element of $S$ annihilates a nonzero element of $R$.],[Compute its kernel by the vanishing criterion.],[An element maps to zero iff some denominator-set element kills it.],[The kernel is $\{a:u a=0 " for some " u in S\}$. It is zero precisely under the stated condition. This equivalence also handles the case $0 in S$, where injectivity fails unless $R$ is zero.],[State both directions and the zero-ring edge case.]),
 p(6,"C","U6.1 Identifying a localization",[Prove localizing $ZZ slash 6ZZ$ at powers of two gives $FF_3$.],[Reduction modulo three inverts two.],[The kernel that disappears is generated by three.],[Reduction gives a map to $FF_3$ sending every power of two to a unit, so it extends to the localization. In that localization three is zero because two kills it. Thus the original map factors through $ZZ slash 3ZZ$, and two is already invertible there. The resulting maps in both directions agree on original elements and inverses of two, so they are inverse.],[Use the universal property to verify the inverse maps.]),
 p(7,"C","U6.2 A common annihilator",[If $M$ is finitely generated and $S^(-1)M=0$, prove some $s in S$ kills all of $M$.],[Choose one annihilator for each generator.],[Multiply the finitely many annihilators.],[For generators $m_1,dots,m_n$, the vanishing criterion gives $s_i in S$ with $s_i m_i=0$. The product $s=product_i s_i$ belongs to $S$ and kills every generator, hence every linear combination. Thus $s M=0$. For the zero module choose $s=1$.],[Use finite generation explicitly.]),
 p(8,"C","U6.2 Quotients and localization",[Show $S^(-1)(M slash N) iso S^(-1)M slash "im"(S^(-1)N)$.],[Localize the quotient exact sequence.],[Exactness identifies the cokernel.],[Localizing $N -> M -> M slash N ->0$ remains exact, so the target is the quotient of $S^(-1)M$ by the image of the localized submodule. Explicitly $m/s$ maps to $(m+N)/s$. Since localization preserves injections, that image can also be identified with $S^(-1)N$.],[Explain both the image formulation and the injective identification.]),
 p(9,"C","U6.1 Local rings",[Show the nonunits in $R_frak(p)$ form its unique maximal ideal.],[Fractions with numerator outside the prime are invertible.],[Fractions with numerator inside it form a proper ideal.],[The numerator-in-$frak(p)$ fractions form a proper ideal by the prime-localization theorem. Every fraction outside it has an invertible numerator and is a unit. No element of a proper ideal can be a unit. Thus this ideal is exactly the nonunits and contains every proper ideal, making it uniquely maximal.],[Use properness as well as the unit calculation.]),
 p(10,"A","U6.2 Detecting zero locally",[Prove an $R$-module $M$ is zero if $M_frak(m)=0$ for every maximal ideal $frak(m)$. No finite-generation hypothesis is allowed.],[If $m!=0$, its annihilator is a proper ideal.],[Choose a maximal ideal containing that annihilator.],[If $m in M$ is nonzero, its annihilator is proper and lies in some maximal ideal $frak(m)$. If $m/1=0$ in that localization, some $s notin frak(m)$ kills $m$, placing $s$ in the annihilator and hence in $frak(m)$, a contradiction. Thus some localization is nonzero. The contrapositive proves the claim.],[Work with one element's annihilator; do not assume a finite generator list.]),
 p(11,"A","U6.1 Localization in stages",[Show inverting $f$ and then $g$ in a commutative ring is equivalent to inverting $f g$.],[Compare the maps each construction permits into a target.],[If $f g$ is a unit, both $f$ and $g$ are units.],[A map out of the iterated localization is exactly a map from $R$ sending both $f,g$ to units. This is equivalent to sending $f g$ to a unit: if $u$ inverts $f g$, then $g u$ inverts $f$ and $f u$ inverts $g$, using commutativity. Thus the two rings have the same universal property and are canonically isomorphic.],[Prove the implication from the product being a unit.]),
 p(12,
  "A",
  "U6.2 Flatness of localization (proof reconstruction)",
  [Prove $S^(-1)R$ is flat by explicitly checking that an injection remains injective after localization and identifying localization with tensoring.],
  [Use a vanishing fraction in the image.],
  [An element of $S$ killing the image must kill the original element by injectivity.],
  [*Finding the approach.* A localized element is zero when some allowed denominator kills its numerator. This is weaker than the numerator being zero and is exactly the right invariant to use.

*Argument.* For injective $f:M -> N$, if $f(m)/s=0$, there is $u in S$ with $f(u m)=0$. Injectivity gives $u m=0$, so $m/s=0$. Hence localization preserves injections. The natural isomorphism $S^(-1)R otimes M iso S^(-1)M$ transfers this property to tensoring with $S^(-1)R$, which is flatness.

The tensor comparison sends $(r/s) otimes m$ to $(r m)/s$. Its inverse sends $m/s$ to $(1/s) otimes m$. The fraction relation and the tensor balancing relation make these formulas well defined, and substituting a pure tensor or a fraction checks the two composites. In the injectivity argument, if the image fraction is zero, obtain a witness $u in S$ before using injectivity of $f$; the result $u m=0$ is precisely the witness needed in the source localization.

*A tempting dead end.* Do not cancel a denominator in the original module or infer $m=0$ from $m/s=0$. Localization deliberately kills S-torsion.

*Hypotheses.* The ring is commutative and $S$ is multiplicatively closed. Flatness concerns preservation of every module injection; one numerical example is not sufficient.],
  [State the tensor identification and the exact vanishing criterion.],
  idea: [Transport the zero-fraction witness across the injection and then use the tensor-localization comparison.]),
 p(13,
  "A",
  "U6 Transfer: Two localized views of a crossing",
  [Let $A=K[x,y] slash (x y)$. Consider the map $A -> K[x,x^(-1)] times K[y,y^(-1)]$ obtained by setting y=0 in the first coordinate and x=0 in the second. Is it injective or surjective? Describe its image exactly.],
  [Reduce a polynomial modulo xy before evaluating either coordinate.],
  [A reduced expression has one constant term, positive powers of x, and positive powers of y.],
  [*Finding the approach.* The relation kills all mixed monomials. A normal form reveals both what each localization sees and the one piece of information the two views must share.

*Argument.* Every element has a unique expression $c+x f(x)+y g(y)$: discard monomials containing both variables, and uniqueness follows because a polynomial in the ideal $(x y)$ has only mixed monomials. Its image is $(c+x f(x),c+y g(y))$. If both coordinates vanish, all their polynomial coefficients vanish, so the original element is zero and the map is injective. Its image consists exactly of pairs of ordinary polynomials in x and y having the same constant term; the displayed expression constructs a preimage for each such pair. It is not surjective, since $(x^(-1),0)$ is not in the image, and even $(1,0)$ violates the equal-constant requirement.

*A tempting dead end.* Two localized descriptions cannot necessarily be chosen independently. Also localization does not mean every tuple of fractions comes from a single unlocalized element.

*Hypotheses.* The relation is exactly xy=0 over a field, so the monomial normal form has no additional identifications. Laurent-polynomial coordinates are the localizations where x or y is inverted.],
  [Prove uniqueness of normal form, injectivity, both directions of the image description, and failure of surjectivity.],
  optional: true,
  minutes: 60,
  idea: [Find a unique normal form before comparing local images; the shared constant measures the compatibility left at the intersection.]),
)
